In article <7616@batcomputer.tn.cornell.edu> mll@honesty.tn.cornell.edu writes:
>When you have a polymorph control ring and are asked what to turn into
>you should only be able to turn into something that you have already
>encountered. e.g. you have already killed one so have had time to examine
>it, or you were randomly polymorphed into one. 

Well, that's an interesting idea.  Reminds me of an AD&D campaign where
my character could only cast credible illusions to mimic spell effects
he'd seen before...

>Another more tentative suggestion is changing the dungeon floorplan
>size to fit the window. Perhaps I should elaborate further. I use an
>xterm to play nethack and would like to see all of the window used if
>I can get one larger than 80x24. 
[ pros and cons deleted ]

Main problem I see with this is if you try to restart a saved game on a
terminal smaller than the one you started on...
Also, this plays a bit with game balance-- I played a version of rogue
which did this, and either you got an excess of magic items/stuff on
each level-- imagine accumulatying a dozen scrolls each time you reach a
new level-- or you ran into trouble with running out of food
because of the greater distances to traverse... I suppose if you scaled
up/down the food given according to the size of the level and kept
things otherwise the same it might work, but this is getting a bit
crazy...

